normal" alt="" title="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FTaS!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4b211e95-bf15-447a-9a02-0e84e60732e8_1198x856.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FTaS!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4b211e95-bf15-447a-9a02-0e84e60732e8_1198x856.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FTaS!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4b211e95-bf15-447a-9a02-0e84e60732e8_1198x856.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!FTaS!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F4b211e95-bf15-447a-9a02-0e84e60732e8_1198x856.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture><div class="image-link-expand"><div class="pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset"><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke="var(--color-fg-primary)" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon-noB79L"><g><path d="M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex="0" type="button" class="pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L"><polyline points="15 3 21 3 21 9"></polyline><polyline points="9 21 3 21 3 15"></polyline><line x1="21" x2="14" y1="3" y2="10"></line><line x1="3" x2="10" y1="21" y2="14"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a><figcaption class="image-caption">Source: Box State of AI in the Enterprise report, 2026</figcaption></figure></div><p>Box surveyed more than 1,600 leaders for its <a href="https://www.box.com/en-gb/state-of-ai?utm_source=newsletter&amp;utm_medium=emeapaidinfluencer&amp;utm_theme=ai&amp;utm_content=substack-sept&amp;utm_campaign=exponential-view&amp;utm_keyword=FY27Q3SEAI_Azeem_1">2026 State of AI report</a>.</p><p>83% of surveyed organizations say they already run AI agents. Four in five report moderate or significant ROI, and half saw business impact within six months of approving a project.</p><p>The agents work, but what varies is how much firms get out of them. The report shows that top adopters put people in charge of agents, sort out the content AI draws on and build systems that adapt as models improve.</p><p>Download the report for data, benchmarks and tips for AI adoption.</p><p class="button-wrapper" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.box.com/en-gb/state-of-ai?utm_source=newsletter&amp;utm_medium=emeapaidinfluencer&amp;utm_theme=ai&amp;utm_content=substack-sept&amp;utm_campaign=exponential-view&amp;utm_keyword=FY27Q3SEAI_Azeem_1&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Download report&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}" data-component-name="ButtonCreateButton"><a class="button primary button-wrapper" href="https://www.box.com/en-gb/state-of-ai?utm_source=newsletter&amp;utm_medium=emeapaidinfluencer&amp;utm_theme=ai&amp;utm_content=substack-sept&amp;utm_campaign=exponential-view&amp;utm_keyword=FY27Q3SEAI_Azeem_1"><span>Download report</span></a></p><div><hr></div><h3>Safety</h3><p>Let&#8217;s turn to recursive self-improvement and the 160-million-plus-view tweet</p><div class="twitter-embed" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://x.com/hilbertspaess/status/2097476196791709843?s=20&quot;,&quot;full_text&quot;:&quot;I resigned from Anthropic today. I spent the last three years doing pretraining research at both OpenAI and Anthropic. Neither company is acting responsibly. They are racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives. More thoughts below.&quot;,&quot;username&quot;:&quot;hilbertspaess&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Jacob Coxon&quot;,&quot;profile_image_url&quot;:&quot;https://pbs.substack.com/profile_images/2097232673865601024/d6H8UbQJ_normal.jpg&quot;,&quot;date&quot;:&quot;2026-09-09T00:04:29.000Z&quot;,&quot;photos&quot;:[],&quot;quoted_tweet&quot;:{},&quot;reply_count&quot;:18924,&quot;retweet_count&quot;:158133,&quot;like_count&quot;:767683,&quot;impression_count&quot;:161671604,&quot;expanded_url&quot;:null,&quot;video_url&quot;:null,&quot;video_preview_media_key&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true}" data-component-name="Twitter2ToDOM"></div><blockquote><p>The people building AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade. This is not a marketing stunt. If anything, many executives and senior researchers will couch their phrasing in the press to sound sensible - but I hear the same people express fear privately. No other human activity poses this level of danger.</p></blockquote><p>These safety concerns were normalised inside the AI community long before the labs themselves were built. Back in 2016, two then-OpenAI employees, Jack Clark and Dario Amodei, wrote that reinforcement learning might be difficult to make safe.</p><div class="twitter-embed" data-attrs="{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://x.com/roybahat/status/2098899630008258934?s=20&quot;,&quot;full_text&quot;:&quot;Meanwhile, two guys at OpenAI worried about these kinds of misaligned reward functions... a decade ago.\n\n(Their names were Dario and Jack.)\n\n&quot;,&quot;username&quot;:&quot;roybahat&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Roy E. Bahat&quot;,&quot;profile_image_url&quot;:&quot;https://pbs.substack.com/profile_images/1339294060864020484/2FujKn6X_normal.jpg&quot;,&quot;date&quot;:&quot;2026-09-12T22:20:42.000Z&quot;,&quot;photos&quot;:[],&quot;quoted_tweet&quot;:{},&quot;reply_count&quot;:0,&quot;retweet_count&quot;:9,&quot;like_count&quot;:110,&quot;impression_count&quot;:15597,&quot;expanded_url&quot;:{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://openai.com/index/faulty-reward-functions/&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Faulty reward functions in the wild&quot;,&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Reinforcement learning algorithms can break in surprising, counterintuitive ways. In this post we&#8217;ll explore one failure mode, which is where you misspecify your reward function.&quot;,&quot;domain&quot;:&quot;openai.com&quot;,&quot;image&quot;:&quot;https://pbs.substack.com/news_img/2098132447577731084/HMaIBGo2?format=jpg&amp;name=orig&quot;},&quot;video_url&quot;:null,&quot;video_preview_media_key&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true}" data-component-name="Twitter2ToDOM"></div><p>When Anthropic goes public, one of the risk factors on its S1 ought to be that reasonably senior executives believe there is a significant chance the company will kill all of humanity. Whether that is good or bad for the company is unclear at this point.</p><p>But the net result has been what can best be described as a coordinated agreement between OpenAI and Anthropic to &#8220;<a href="https://darioamodei.com/post/we-must-pace-the-frontier">pace the frontier</a>&#8221;, as Amodei put it. Altman <a href="https://x.com/sama/status/2098811563415150910">agreed</a>. The proposals would include giving independent evaluators employee-level access to internal systems.</p><p>OpenAI and two of Anthropic&#8217;s cofounders have known about the problem of aligning RL-based systems for a decade. They have since become oligopolistic powers in an emerging industry. They have brand recognition, capital depth, technical momentum and resources. And now they realise they need to collaborate to slow down technical development (and by extension, raise the cost of entry for future competitors)?</p><p>I&#8217;m in Edinburgh this weekend, and I walked past Adam Smith&#8217;s grave yesterday. This brought to mind the philosopher&#8217;s remarks in <em>The Wealth of Nations</em>, </p><blockquote><p>People of the same trade seldom meet together &#8230; but the conversation ends in a conspiracy against the public, or in some contrivance to raise prices.</p></blockquote>
      <p>
